WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.451
NARRATOR: Algorithm analysis begins by
separating three layers.

2
00:00:04.451 --> 00:00:09.081
NARRATOR: The problem states the required
relationship between inputs and outputs.

3
00:00:09.081 --> 00:00:12.869
NARRATOR: An algorithm is a
language-independent procedure for producing

4
00:00:12.869 --> 00:00:13.711
the output.

5
00:00:13.711 --> 00:00:18.922
NARRATOR: An implementation expresses that
procedure in a programming language and on a

6
00:00:18.922 --> 00:00:19.323
machine.

7
00:00:19.323 --> 00:00:25.261
NARRATOR: Two programs may implement the same
algorithm, and two algorithms may solve the

8
00:00:25.261 --> 00:00:27.806
same problem with very different costs.

9
00:00:27.806 --> 00:00:33.398
NARRATOR: State assumptions about input size,
allowed operations, and required correctness

10
00:00:33.398 --> 00:00:34.923
before comparing performance.

11
00:00:34.923 --> 00:00:40.102
NARRATOR: Otherwise a faster-looking program
may be solving an easier problem or relying on

12
00:00:40.102 --> 00:00:42.691
an operation whose cost has been ignored.

13
00:00:42.691 --> 00:00:47.576
NARRATOR: Analysis makes comparisons
meaningful by fixing the question first.

14
00:00:47.690 --> 00:00:51.160
NARRATOR: Performance cannot rescue an
incorrect result.

15
00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:56.994
NARRATOR: A correctness argument begins with
preconditions describing allowed inputs and a

16
00:00:56.994 --> 00:00:59.425
postcondition describing the required output.

17
00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:04.404
NARRATOR: For iterative algorithms, a loop
invariant connects the work already completed

18
00:01:04.404 --> 00:01:06.479
to the work that remains.

19
00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:12.156
NARRATOR: Show that the invariant is true
initially, preserved by each iteration, and

20
00:01:12.156 --> 00:01:15.650
strong enough to imply the postcondition at
termination.

21
00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:21.934
NARRATOR: For recursive algorithms, prove the
base case and show that correct solutions to

22
00:01:21.934 --> 00:01:25.076
smaller subproblems produce a correct larger
solution.

23
00:01:25.076 --> 00:01:29.650
NARRATOR: Test examples help find
counterexamples, but a proof explains why

24
00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:31.313
every allowed case works.

25
00:01:31.313 --> 00:01:36.262
NARRATOR: Correctness and performance are
separate claims, and both need evidence.

26
00:01:36.386 --> 00:01:39.167
NARRATOR: To analyze cost, define n.

27
00:01:39.167 --> 00:01:44.542
NARRATOR: It may be the number of array
elements, graph vertices and edges, digits in

28
00:01:44.542 --> 00:01:47.050
an integer, or dimensions of a matrix.

29
00:01:47.050 --> 00:01:52.037
NARRATOR: Then define which operations count
as constant time in the chosen model.

30
00:01:52.037 --> 00:01:56.624
NARRATOR: Array access and machine-word
arithmetic may be constant under a

31
00:01:56.624 --> 00:02:01.629
random-access model, but arithmetic on
integers with thousands of digits is not.

32
00:02:01.629 --> 00:02:06.977
NARRATOR: Hash-table lookup is commonly
expected constant time under assumptions, not

33
00:02:06.977 --> 00:02:09.894
guaranteed constant time for every input.

34
00:02:09.894 --> 00:02:11.489
NARRATOR: State these choices.

35
00:02:11.489 --> 00:02:16.297
NARRATOR: An asymptotic result without an
input measure or model is incomplete.

36
00:02:16.297 --> 00:02:22.223
NARRATOR: The model is an abstraction, but a
useful abstraction exposes dominant structure

37
00:02:22.223 --> 00:02:24.958
while remaining connected to implementation
reality.

38
00:02:25.106 --> 00:02:30.298
NARRATOR: Asymptotic notation describes growth
beyond a sufficiently large input.

39
00:02:30.298 --> 00:02:36.617
NARRATOR: Big O is an upper bound: after some
threshold, the cost is no more than a constant

40
00:02:36.617 --> 00:02:38.372
multiple of the comparison function.

41
00:02:38.372 --> 00:02:40.247
NARRATOR: Omega is a lower bound.

42
00:02:40.247 --> 00:02:44.175
NARRATOR: Theta states both and therefore
gives a tight growth class.

43
00:02:44.175 --> 00:02:49.905
NARRATOR: Big O is not automatically worst
case; worst, average, and best case describe

44
00:02:49.905 --> 00:02:53.589
which inputs are considered, while O describes
a bound.

45
00:02:53.589 --> 00:03:00.822
NARRATOR: A function in Theta of n is also in
O of n squared, but the looser statement hides

46
00:03:00.822 --> 00:03:01.203
information.

47
00:03:01.203 --> 00:03:06.854
NARRATOR: Use the tightest justified class and
state which case is being analyzed.

48
00:03:06.854 --> 00:03:12.143
NARRATOR: Constants and lower-order terms
disappear asymptotically, not because they

49
00:03:12.143 --> 00:03:15.846
never matter, but because growth eventually
dominates.

50
00:03:15.986 --> 00:03:22.160
NARRATOR: Constant, logarithmic, linear, n log
n, quadratic, polynomial, and exponential

51
00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:24.966
growth describe very different scaling.

52
00:03:24.966 --> 00:03:30.156
NARRATOR: Doubling n barely changes a
logarithmic cost, doubles a linear cost,

53
00:03:30.156 --> 00:03:37.076
roughly doubles plus an additional factor for
n log n, quadruples a quadratic cost, and can

54
00:03:37.076 --> 00:03:39.239
multiply an exponential cost enormously.

55
00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:43.780
NARRATOR: This ordering guides design choices
before low-level optimization.

56
00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:49.490
NARRATOR: A carefully optimized quadratic
algorithm can beat a linear one on tiny

57
00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:53.882
inputs, but as n grows the growth-rate
advantage eventually dominates.

58
00:03:53.882 --> 00:03:58.078
NARRATOR: Use realistic input ranges and
memory constraints too.

59
00:03:58.078 --> 00:04:03.160
NARRATOR: Asymptotics predict scaling; they do
not replace measurement or permit ignoring

60
00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:06.548
large constants when the deployment range is
known.

61
00:04:06.674 --> 00:04:11.203
NARRATOR: For a loop, count how many times the
body runs and the cost of each run.

62
00:04:11.203 --> 00:04:16.228
NARRATOR: A loop from zero to n minus one with
constant body work is linear.

63
00:04:16.228 --> 00:04:22.095
NARRATOR: Two independent loops in sequence
add, so linear plus linear remains linear.

64
00:04:22.095 --> 00:04:27.478
NARRATOR: Perfectly nested loops of n
iterations multiply to n squared.

65
00:04:27.478 --> 00:04:33.842
NARRATOR: But bounds may depend on the outer
index, producing a sum such as one plus two

66
00:04:33.842 --> 00:04:36.088
through n, still Theta n squared.

67
00:04:36.088 --> 00:04:40.412
NARRATOR: A loop that repeatedly halves the
remaining size is logarithmic.

68
00:04:40.412 --> 00:04:44.506
NARRATOR: Read the update and bounds rather
than counting indentation.

69
00:04:44.506 --> 00:04:48.371
NARRATOR: If the body calls another algorithm,
include that cost.

70
00:04:48.371 --> 00:04:53.805
NARRATOR: Writing the sum explicitly often
turns intuition into a defensible result.

71
00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:58.012
NARRATOR: A recursive algorithm's cost can be
modeled with a recurrence.

72
00:04:58.012 --> 00:05:03.369
NARRATOR: Merge sort divides an input into two
halves, recursively sorts each half, and

73
00:05:03.369 --> 00:05:09.874
performs linear merging, giving T of n equals
two T of n over two plus Theta n.

74
00:05:09.874 --> 00:05:14.556
NARRATOR: A recursion tree shows
logarithmically many levels with linear total

75
00:05:14.556 --> 00:05:17.961
work per level, producing Theta n log n.

76
00:05:17.961 --> 00:05:22.877
NARRATOR: Other recurrences behave differently
depending on the number and size of

77
00:05:22.877 --> 00:05:24.925
subproblems and the combination cost.

78
00:05:24.925 --> 00:05:30.056
NARRATOR: State a base case and avoid applying
a memorized theorem without checking its

79
00:05:30.056 --> 00:05:30.423
conditions.

80
00:05:30.423 --> 00:05:35.761
NARRATOR: Recurrences are not only algebra;
they mirror the structure of the algorithm and

81
00:05:35.761 --> 00:05:37.668
reveal where the work accumulates.

82
00:05:37.802 --> 00:05:42.190
NARRATOR: An algorithm is often a sequence of
data-structure operations.

83
00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:47.863
NARRATOR: Searching an unsorted array is
linear, while a balanced search tree can

84
00:05:47.863 --> 00:05:50.481
support logarithmic lookup and ordered
traversal.

85
00:05:50.481 --> 00:05:55.109
NARRATOR: A hash table can provide expected
constant-time lookup under assumptions but

86
00:05:55.109 --> 00:05:57.037
does not preserve sorted order.

87
00:05:57.037 --> 00:06:01.894
NARRATOR: A heap makes repeated minimum
extraction efficient without supporting every

88
00:06:01.894 --> 00:06:02.777
search efficiently.

89
00:06:02.777 --> 00:06:07.313
NARRATOR: The right structure follows the
operations the problem performs most often,

90
00:06:07.313 --> 00:06:11.093
the guarantees it requires, and the memory it
can afford.

91
00:06:11.093 --> 00:06:14.690
NARRATOR: Include construction cost, not just
query cost.

92
00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:20.366
NARRATOR: A structure that accelerates one
operation may slow updates or use more space.

93
00:06:20.366 --> 00:06:25.098
NARRATOR: Time-space tradeoffs are algorithmic
choices, not afterthoughts.

94
00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:30.365
NARRATOR: Benchmarks reveal constants, cache
behavior, interpreter overhead, input

95
00:06:30.365 --> 00:06:35.480
distributions, and implementation defects that
an abstract model omits.

96
00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:41.880
NARRATOR: Measure representative inputs, vary
n systematically, repeat trials, and record

97
00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:43.044
the environment.

98
00:06:43.044 --> 00:06:48.197
NARRATOR: Avoid timing unrelated setup unless
it is part of the operation being evaluated.

99
00:06:48.197 --> 00:06:53.044
NARRATOR: Plot time against candidate growth
functions and investigate discrepancies.

100
00:06:53.044 --> 00:06:59.428
NARRATOR: A benchmark on five sizes cannot
prove asymptotic complexity, and an asymptotic

101
00:06:59.428 --> 00:07:03.847
proof cannot predict an exact runtime on one
laptop.

102
00:07:03.847 --> 00:07:05.876
NARRATOR: Use both forms of evidence.

103
00:07:05.876 --> 00:07:11.499
NARRATOR: Analysis explains expected scaling
under a model; measurement checks how a

104
00:07:11.499 --> 00:07:14.779
particular implementation and workload behave
in reality.

105
00:07:14.906 --> 00:07:18.287
NARRATOR: A complete analysis answers eight
questions.

106
00:07:18.287 --> 00:07:21.514
NARRATOR: What problem and input domain are
being solved?

107
00:07:21.514 --> 00:07:23.287
NARRATOR: Why is the algorithm correct?

108
00:07:23.287 --> 00:07:26.016
NARRATOR: What quantity defines input size?

109
00:07:26.016 --> 00:07:28.733
NARRATOR: Which model assigns costs to
operations?

110
00:07:28.733 --> 00:07:34.958
NARRATOR: Are we discussing worst, average,
expected, amortized, or best case?

111
00:07:34.958 --> 00:07:37.343
NARRATOR: What tight time and space bounds
follow?

112
00:07:37.343 --> 00:07:40.850
NARRATOR: Which data-structure assumptions
support those bounds?

113
00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:43.861
NARRATOR: What do measurements show over the
intended range?

114
00:07:43.861 --> 00:07:48.019
NARRATOR: This checklist prevents Big O from
becoming a decorative label.

115
00:07:48.019 --> 00:07:53.586
NARRATOR: The goal is an argument another
person can inspect: the algorithm returns the

116
00:07:53.586 --> 00:07:58.358
right answer, the derivation follows its
structure, the assumptions are visible, and

117
00:07:58.358 --> 00:08:01.539
the measured implementation behaves
consistently with the claim.
